        "identifier": "https://github.com/DiarHaidary/Sharp-Bounds-for-Graph-Matrices/tree/01994362f8543743d556e7544d2538e93c62c02b",
        "note": "Earlier Lean development by the same author, cited at the exact public commit DiarHaidary/Sharp-Bounds-for-Graph-Matrices@01994362f8543743d556e7544d2538e93c62c02b (MIT). It is a prior formalization reused as a Lean dependency, not a mathematical source: its own subject, sharp norm bounds for fixed-shape graph matrices, is unrelated to MI-32 and none of its graph-matrix results are reused or re-claimed here. What is reused is its scalar Boolean machinery. The necessary source closure of GraphMatrices.WalshFourthMoment is vendored byte-identically into vendor/graph-matrices/ with its original notices, its MIT licence and per-file SHA-256 hashes in vendor/graph-matrices/manifest.json; every one of those 16 files is byte-identical to that commit. The single load-bearing reused theorem is GraphMatrices.Walsh.polynomial_fourth_le_nine_pow, the scalar Boolean two-to-four fourth-moment inequality with constant 9^d, together with the supporting Walsh polynomial, independent-sign law and finite-law vocabulary. What this development adds on top of it is MI32.hilbert_walsh_fourth_le in MI32/HilbertWalsh.lean, a dimension-free finite-dimensional real Hilbert-space extension of that inequality for a family of Walsh polynomials with arbitrary real coefficients, proved here from the scalar case and a pairwise Cauchy-Schwarz argument. The vendored closure is broader than the part used, because of the upstream module import structure.",
